> `offset(date)`: count working days from origin (exclusive of origin) up to date. If date <= origin, returns 0 or negative count. Let me define: number of working days in (origin, date]. For date == origin → 0. For the next working day → 1.
> 
> Implementation:
> ```php
> public function offset(CarbonInterface $date): int
> {
>     $cursor = $this->origin->copy()->startOfDay();
>     $target = $date->copy()->startOfDay();
>     $sign = $target->greaterThanOrEqualTo($cursor) ? 1 : -1;
>     $count = 0;
>     while (! $cursor->equalTo($target)) {
>         $cursor = $cursor->addDays($sign);
>         if ($this->isWorkingDay($cursor)) {
>             $count += $sign;
>         }
>     }
>     return $count;
> }
> ```
> That's O(n) per call — fine for small projects but called a lot. Since projects are small (< 100 days), it's fine. But to be safe, let me implement a formula-based version:
> 
> Working days between two dates = (full weeks * 5) + remainder weekdays.
> 
> ```php
> public function offset(CarbonInterface $date): int
> {
>     return $this->workingDaysBetween($this->origin, $date);
> }
> 
> private function workingDaysBetween(CarbonInterface $from, CarbonInterface $to): int
> {
>     $from = $from->copy()->startOfDay();
>     $to = $to->copy()->startOfDay();
>     if ($from->equalTo($to)) return 0;
>     $sign = $to->greaterThan($from) ? 1 : -1;
>     if ($sign < 0) { [$from, $to] = [$to, $from]; }
>     // count working days in ($from, $to]  -- i.e., excluding $from
>     $days = $from->diffInDays($to);
>     $fullWeeks = intdiv($days, 7);
>     $count = $fullWeeks * 5;
>     $remainder = $days % 7;
>     $cursor = $from->copy()->addDays($fullWeeks * 7);
>     for ($i = 0; $i < $remainder; $i++) {
>         $cursor = $cursor->addDays(1);
>         if ($this->isWorkingDay($cursor)) $count++;
>     }
>     return $sign * $count;
> }
> ```
